th just  saying a little bit about the analytical  challenge that this project presented  for the clinical chemistry labs  so at the moment virtually all clinical  mass effect work in the uk is small  molecule work we offer quantitative  assays which we  hope are accurate precise and selective  and we measure the concentration of an  analyte in a fixed amount of biological  material be that plasma urine or a dry  bloodspot most of the analytes that we  measure are present at around 0.1 to 100  micromolar concentration and although  most lcms methods are limited somewhat  by the lack of automation and  pre-analytics they are typically  considered to be the gold standard  method of analysis so proteomics was an  entirely new field for us we normally  precipitate the protein and keep the  supernatant and all of a sudden we were  doing the reverse and to be honest it  did require a little bit of getting your  head around so we were measuring  peptides but because we were working  with swab samples we had no idea how  much biological material we had started  with so we couldn't report an actual  concentration all we could really do was  determine the amount of material that  we'd injected onto the column and this  again was an entirely unfamiliar concept  to me  and of course the concentration range  that we were trying to measure at  literally blew my mind  we were looking at concentrations that  were six to seven orders of magnitude  lower than anything i'd ever known a  triple quad do  but i think for me one of the best  things about this project was that in  december last year we got mass specs  with this kind of capability installed  into my lab at st thomas's and here they  are so these were my two shiny new water  zebo tqxss  um i named them obviously um and i call  them frank and leslie and this is after  frank pick and leslie green of london  underground fame  so frank was the chief executive of  london passenger transport board and  leslie was the architect who