ting me to talk today my day job is  actually metabolic biochemistry and  newborn screening so virtually all the  tests we do in my lab involve  chromatography or mass spec in some way  shape or form so when in the middle of a  pandemic last year the department of  health were looking for clinical labs  with mass spec experience we fitted the  bill nicely and we were invited to  participate in the moonshot project  this was a project set up in summer 2020  when there was concern over the uk's  capacity for covet testing and the aim  of the moonshot project was to identify  alternative  technologies to pcr for detecting covid  so my talk is kind of going to be in two  halves in the first half i'm going to  talk about the moonshot project itself  and then the second half will focus on  some of the developments which have  occurred since then so i think it's  probably just worth reminding everyone  what the definition of a moonshot is  because i think it helps to remind  ourselves of the context in which this  project originated and just how  ambitious it really was  so a moonshot is an ambitious  exploratory or groundbreaking project  undertaken without any expectation of  near-term profitability or benefit and  without a full investigation of the  potential risks and benefits  so the moonshot project was set up by  the department of health in summer last  year and it was a unique collaboration  between academia industry and the nhs  the academics were tasked with  developing an aspect method for sales  cop2 testing and the nhs clinical labs  were then tasked with operationalizing  the method undertaking a formal method  comparison and validating the method to  iso 15189 standards and of course the  entire process was supported by our  industry partners  so the academics involved with moonshop  were purchased of aaron from manchester  paul skip from southampton don jones  from leicester and kevin mills from  london and all of these people are  amazing scientists and they work  together to come up with this method in  a very short space of time  they've been given set performance  criteria by the department of health  that they had to meet so the method  needed to detect between two and five  target peptides from either the nuclear  captured or the spike protein  it also needed to have 95 sensitivity  and specificity at a ct of around 25 to  30 and it needed to be robust and lend  itself to scale up  so all in all i think you will probably  agree that the specification was in  keeping with the definition of a moon  shot  but these guys are really good and they  came up with version one of the method  by december last year and it quickly  became evident though that with just the  sp in place we didn't have the required  sensitivity  we were also struggling to get hold of  positive samples to play with and given  that we were in the middle of a global  pandemic that was really quite ironic  however they were undeterred and by  january this year they'd introduced cis  kappa into the workflow  so ciscapper stands for stable isotope  standard capture antipeptide antibody  and the ciscapper really boosted the  sensitivity of the method quite  significantly and so it was ready to be  translated into the clinical  laboratories so i'll just give you a  brief overview of the method that the  academic labs developed  so the swap was deactivated at the point  of collection by collecting the sample  into an ethanol buffer now this was  really quite an elegant solution because  neither none of the academic or clinical  labs involved in the moonshot process  had access to category 3 facilities to  deactivate the samples so this was quite  important  the method required both the swab and  the ethanol buffer it was collected in  to be processed so we referred to the  ethanol buffer as a swab juice and we  needed to process the swab as well  because the ethanol fixed about half of  the viral material onto the swab  so the method followed a fairly 