th just  saying a little bit about the analytical  challenge that this project presented  for the clinical chemistry labs  so at the moment virtually all clinical  mass effect work in the uk is small  molecule work we offer quantitative  assays which we  hope are accurate precise and selective  and we measure the concentration of an  analyte in a fixed amount of biological  material be that plasma urine or a dry  bloodspot most of the analytes that we  measure are present at around 0.1 to 100  micromolar concentration and although  most lcms methods are limited somewhat  by the lack of automation and  pre-analytics they are typically  considered to be the gold standard  method of analysis so proteomics was an  entirely new field for us we normally  precipitate the protein and keep the  supernatant and all of a sudden we were  doing the reverse and to be honest it  did require a little bit of getting your  head around so we were measuring  peptides but because we were working  with swab samples we had no idea how  much biological material we had started  with so we couldn't report an actual  concentration all we could really do was  determine the amount of material that  we'd injected onto the column and this  again was an entirely unfamiliar concept  to me  and of course the concentration range  that we were trying to measure at  literally blew my mind  we were looking at concentrations that  were six to seven orders of magnitude  lower than anything i'd ever known a  triple quad do  but i think for me one of the best  things about this project was that in  december last year we got mass specs  with this kind of capability installed  into my lab at st thomas's and here they  are so these were my two shiny new water  zebo tqxss  um i named them obviously um and i call  them frank and leslie and this is after  frank pick and leslie green of london  underground fame  so frank was the chief executive of  london passenger transport board and  leslie was the architect who was  responsible for the characteristic  oxblood reds tube stations and the  really lovely platform tiling patterns  which some of you might be familiar with  but frank and leslie were creative  strategic and visionary  and i think that was really just like  the moonshot project was so it seemed  like a good name for them  so thanks to our industry partners we  had the kit and thanks to the academic  labs we had a method  so our job as clinical labs was then to  solve optimize the method to make it  work in the routine clinical environment  and do a massive comparison with pcr and  validate the method to iso 15189  standards so we took um a fairly  evidence-based approach to this and we  did make some minor tweaks to the method  but primarily we focused on identifying  a calibration strategy  introducing quality control  and working out how to determine whether  a result was positive or negative  and once we'd done this we did a fairly  straightforward method validation and  then the method comparison with pcr  samples  which of course pcr is considered to be  the gold standard method of analysis for  coping testing  um and it's probably also just worth  mentioning that the other thing we did  was to firmly badge the method as  qualitative and although from the ucas  perspective it would be classed as  semi-quantitative because we are  generating numerical results to assist  with the classification in my opinion  the method is essentially qualitative so  i'll just take you through the  highlights of the method validation now  so um we dropped the dji peptide from  the method quite early on because we  found that there were significant issues  with carryover and it also lacked the  sensitivity of the other two peptides  but as a learning point it is always  useful to include a number of target  peptides when you're starting to develop  a method so you can work out which ones  perform best as you go along so luckily  the other two peptides ayn and ade  performed well and we found that they  had acceptable sensitivity  and precision and negligible carryover  we also found that the method was  selective there was no cross-reactivity  from influenza a influenza b or rsb  and until the delta variant came along  the target peptides were unaffected by  the mutations so however the um the  delta variant did result in a mutation  on the ade peptide and this slide is  just trying to describe this visually  for you  so these um in highlighted in green here  you can see the three target peptides  that we've been looking at the dgi the  ayn and the ade but the delta variant  resulted in eight different point  mutations and these are highlighted in  the peptide sequence in red  and the d377y mutation  was um on the ade target peptide and  that's circled and what happened to it  was that the ade became aye in the delta  so this mutation reduced the affinity of  the ciscapper for the ade peptide or  should i say for the aye peptides but  luckily only by approximately 20 percent  so by adding in the mrms for aye into  the mass effect method we were still  able to detect delta variant which was a  real bonus so we did a method comparison  against the thermo fischer  tacpath pcr assay and we compared swabs  from 396 subjects which were collected  as part of the falcon study  and these samples were collected from  people in hospital by healthcare  professionals but also by people  screening themselves at home as part of  the population testing program  we had to exclude 35 samples due to  either poor sample quality or an  inconclusive pcr result which left us  with 361 in the final method comparison  and we found that the mass spec method  had a sensitivity of 92.4  and a specificity of 97 compared with  pcr  and that gave a positive predictive  value of 96.9 percent and cohen's kappa  um demonstrated that there was excellent  agreement between the methods which was  really good  um i think it's also worth pointing out  here that the pcr and mass spec results  were coming from different swab samples  so the efficacy of the swap sampling  process will also be impacting on the  comparison between the two  and to try to put that in a little bit  of context a comparison of just over 500  paired swab samples which were measured  both measured by pcr the same pcr method  but in different labs that gave an error  rate of three percent  um and because there is um a lot of  discussion and perhaps a lack of clarity  around what a ct value um above 32  actually means in terms of someone's  infection we did split out  those samples with ct of greater than 32  and just have a look at what that did to  the sensitivity and as you would expect  it did improve it significantly  as to whether or not that's an  acceptable thing to do i think i would  leave that to um our virologists to  comment on so i think in terms of the  original mask the moonshot project was  really successful  an lcms method had been developed very  quickly and translated into the clinical  lab where it's been validated and both  the analytical performance and the  comparison with pcr  were acceptable however  the method did also have some  limitations which meant it wasn't really  that suitable for the routine clinical  laboratory  i think for me the main one was the  bespoke sample collection tube so  although it was ingenious in its own way  it wasn't logistically feasible and in  fact it made access to patient samples  incredibly difficult  and the table shown here on the slide  just gives you an idea of just how many  samples were received in an incorrect  volume of ethanol which is a reflection  of the tubes being prepared manually and  not through a kind of commercial and  control process and also because both  the swab and the swab juice had to be  processed the method was really a  one-shot method and which obviously  isn't ideal in the routine lab because  you can never go back you just got one  chance to get it right  and again processing the swap without  automation was quite tricky and perhaps  more importantly there was a real risk  of cross-contamination from doing that  um and then on top of that the method  was incredibly labor-intensive and  time-consuming so the mass spec aspect  was really pretty fast but the rate  limiting step was the manual sample  preparation and which really did mean  that if you were doing this manually  without any automation you could really  only prep 20 samples in a day so i think  for me for the method to be suitable for  use in a routine clinical lab the first  thing that needed to change was a sample  type i mean the world and his wife were  collecting swaps for covid into btm and  we needed the method to work on btm we  also needed the process to be automated  partly for ease and simplicity but also  so the throughput was increased and i  think ideally we needed a  post-analytical data processing solution  because the existing process  required review of the numerical data  and visual inspection quite careful  visual inspection of every single  chromatogram so i think really this is  where our industry partners came into  their own so my lab had been partnered  with waters and we've been having some  ongoing discussions about how the method  might work with btm samples but progress  in actually doing something was slow  partly because of the difficulty of  getting hold of reagents so the cis  kappa um was like gold dust in january  february time this year  but also getting hold of positive  samples was quite difficult and this did  slow down our progress but walters came  up with a reagent kit which was designed  to work with btm samples which solved  two of our problems straight away and  then they also loaned us a benchtop  robot the andrews alliance to help with  sample preparation and this had such a  positive impact we also sent um our swap  data to them and hans vittez um was busy  working has been busy working on an  algorithm to try and classify the  samples and i was really pleased a  couple of months ago when he came back  and had independently verified the  classifications that we've done manually  during the validation here's a nice  picture of the um the saskov2 reagent  kit from waters  so the kit method is really similar to  the ethanol-based method i've just been  describing to you but it does have some  important differences  so this kit has three target peptides  the ayn and the ade that we described  previously but it also has another  peptide npa which is also derived from  the nuclear capsid protein  all the reagents including the ciscapper  are provided in the kit  um the lc method is a little bit shorter  it's two minutes and that's because the  dji peptide is no longer included  and most importantly for me the method  is intended to be used with a swab  collected into vtm  so as i'm sure you can imagine i was  really keen to get my hands on some kits  and try and do some validation work so  this meant i needed to find some patient  samples  and luckily with the help of some  colleagues in the emergency department i  was able to obtain ethics to collect  samples at st thomas's  so our inclusion criteria was very  straightforward any adult presenting to  the emergency department between june  and august this year with symptoms of  consistent with covid and who consented  to provide us with a second swab  so that swab sample was sent to our  virology department and they took  responsibility for anonymizing the  samples deactivating them and preparing  two aliquots they kept one electron for  pcr um and then they sent one aliquot to  us for mass spec so of course a major  benefit of this comparison um was that  the pcr would have been done on the  exact same sample as the mass effect so  that would just rule out any of those  potential differences due to the  efficacy of the sampling process and by  the beginning of september we had just  over 300 samples to validate the kit on  so obviously again we did 