samples was five  and a quarter hours and with a contact  time of about two and a quarter hours  however  waters were able to show that the um  btm method only required two wash steps  of the cyst capper so that meant that  the andrews could prep 96 samples in a  time which was a real game changer  so the total time was still about five  and a quarter hours but the contact time  was reduced to one hour 30 minutes and  one hour of that was um me plating out  the samples and ready to go onto the  andrews so i'm just going to give walk  you through the highlights of the um the  validation that we did with the waters  kick now so obviously we looked at  linearity  um and this slide shows the calibration  curves for each peptide so these  calibrations are based on synthetic  peptides not on encap  and the peptides have been taken through  the sample preparation process even  though technically they didn't need to  be just for completeness and to ensure  that they were i guess matrix matched we  did um have to be honest going forward i  don't think there's any merit in doing  this and i think i in future i would  just run them as offline cows um and it  was also probably worth just clarifying  that the aye peptide has been calibrated  against the ade so the table here  summarizes the r squared values and the  back calculated concentrations for  calibrations over five days and i think  you can  i hope you can see that the ayn and ade  performed quite well but the npa a  little bit less so and we found that the  mpa wasn't particularly stable  and it was also considerably less  sensitive than the other two peptides so  calcu for npa shown here is actually at  concentration 10 times higher  than that of the other two peptides um  in terms of imprecision we made some um  three pools qc samples um  that were made up from patient swabs  collected into btm so we had a negative  pool a low positive pool and high  positive cool  unfortunately because of issues with  access to samples we were only able to  obtain one qc for ade and that was a  weak positive pool  um we found the assay was reasonably  precise the interbat imprecision was  around 10 in the high pool and around 20  in the low positive pool and so  obviously if you're used to quantitative  essays that perhaps doesn't sound like  brilliant performance but i just think  bearing in mind the concentrations that  we're um looking at here and the fact  that this is really a qualitative assay  i think that's reasonable performance  and i think we can be confident in that  we also found that the assay was  selective um we analyzed 18 patient  samples which were positive for one of  the four other seasonal corona viruses  and found no cross-reactivity with um  covid  so that was good and we also looked at  spike's recombinant end cap which with  increasing concentrations of influenza a  influenza b and rsv and again there was  no cross reactivity at all so i'm just  going to show you some example  chromatograms so you have  an idea of the kind of data that we were  getting from this method  so these are the um extracted ion  current chromatograms from a negative  sample  um each peptide has a quantifier and at  least one qualifier iron and of course  there's a stabilized text standard  included as well so on the top here  we've got a y e x ay n sorry in the  middle we've got ade and aye  and npa's at the bottom and for each  peptide you can see here just glancing  at it that only the stable isotopes are  present so this was a true negative  the next one we've got an example of is  a true positive result so again ayn is  at the top  a d e and a y e are in the middle and  mpa is at the bottom  and this time you can see that in  addition to the stable isotope ayn and  both its qualifier ions are present  aye and it's qualified iron are present  and at the bottom you can see that we've  got the mpa and both its qualifier ions  so this is a bond or positive and  obviously it's also a delta variant so  this next slide is just summarizing the  concentration of the aym peptide for  samples that were pcr negative and pcr  positive um the pcr-positive samples  have been split into those with a  takeoff value  of greater than 20 so in other words the  low positives and those with a takeoff  value of less than 20 which obviously  are the um the more straightforward  positives  and what this slide is really showing  you that there is no really clear  discrimination between the um a positive  and a negative in terms of absolute  concentration and again this is the same  um the same information but for the  other two peptides aye and mpa and again  you can see that there isn't absolute  discrimination between a positive and a  negative they've also been um  looking at the data to see if we can  extract any other information out of it  so had to look at whether the how the  ayn  correlates with the take-off value and  you can see that there's broad  correlation as you would expect  but not particularly good correlation i  think the one that correlates the best  is actually aye it's got an r squared  value of 0.779 though so it's not really  brilliant  also been looking at ratios um this box  whisker plot just shows um the ratio of  the aui and the other two peptides and  we were looking in case it added some  discriminatory value but again you can  see for both peptides the range is  actually quite large so we'll continue  to go through the data and see if we can  um draw anything else out from it and  obviously i think it's probably worth  mentioning that at some point in the  next couple of weeks i'll be sending  all this data on to waters as well to  see if hands can do anything um more  sophisticated with it in terms of um  the sensitivity and specificity um this  is probably the most interesting bit  of the 325 swabs we managed to collect  from the symptoms emergency department  we had to exclude 10 because there was  either insufficient sample volume  or there was an inconclusive pcr result  and that left 315 in the final  comparison and we found that the mass  spec method had a sensitivity of 75  and a specificity of 100 compared with  pcr  again cohen's kappa um was greater than  0.83  which is consistent with excellent  agreement between the methods  we also looked at the um those samples  were the takeoff  um of less than 20  as a separate group and you can see that  that increased the sensitivities um  quite significantly to 92.9 percent so i  think um in conclusion the waters  sasquatch ii kit has been validated on  vtm patient samples it's got acceptable  analytical performance and we also found  the method to be very specific it's not  going to give false positive which is a  definite advantage and i think in terms  of sensitivity um it performs well at  the lower take-off values and although  the higher take-off values its  performance isn't as great it's not  actually um it's it's comparable to  other antigen tests that are available  and used routinely such as the rosh  alexis assay  so  i think um  what we've also shown is that if the  process is automated it could be  suitable for a routine clinical  environment  but i think for me um  what risk is really doing is kind of  demonstrating the art of the possible  um  i think the moonshot project has  demonstrated what can be achieved and  just how quickly if industry academia  and the nhs work together in partnership  and i think that's really important i  think it's also highlighted the benefits  of investing in high-end kit in clinical  laboratories  um but again for me i think the real  take-home message is that clinical labs  can do peptide analysis we've got the  kit and we've got a workflow  and for me that means we have a whole  new set of biomarkers available to us  and the question really is just which  one we want to start with  so obviously moonshot has been a massive  collaborative project  and there are loads of people that have  been involved and need to be  acknowledged um i obviously haven't  captured them all on this slide but i  very much hope i've got the um the key  players  so there's the rest of my team in  biopath  in particular kate john and kaylee davis  who have been helping on the lab side  and penny cliff who is my contact and um  very friendly virologist  and then kevin mills and his team from  the institute of child health they were  our partner p1 laboratory um and they  were fabulous to work with and obviously  then there's the team from waters  and then those people that were working  directly at the department of health  i'll stop there and if anybody would  like to ask any questions i'll be more  than happy to try to answer them thank  you  so there was a question about the  take-home messages in terms of the  peptide assays and i think for us it was  the need to include multiple target  peptides  during the validation process and also  particularly for the kovitch we didn't  have a marker of swab integrity in there  which was unfortunate because our  results would perhaps have been a little  bit more easy to interpret but because  we were using assist kappa that actually  prevented us from going back and adding  in um a potential marker in real time so  i think that's something to bear in mind  so there is massive advantages of the  cisco in terms of sensitivity um but it  does mean you can't just add additional  things into your assay which um which is  fine but i think the lead time forces  kappa to produce new antibodies is  between six and nine months so it's just  something to bear in mind  i'm just going to have a look and see if  there's been any more questions  notes there's just um  one other question that i've got in the  chat  um about what we what else we learned  during the essay so we're actually in  the process of doing a formal lessons  learned um review which is something  we're quite keen on in england  sorry about the background noise um  so we've just contacted everyone who's  involved in moonshot um with a  questionnaire um asking their thoughts  on what went well how we can improve  things in the future so that if we ever  do do another collaborative project in  this way we will be able to focus in on  what worked well and avoid some of the  areas where we perhaps made mistakes and  which i think is important because there  were so many people involved it was  difficult to keep track of where  everybody was at some point in time  i'm just checking the chat i don't think  there's any more questions  so i guess i'll hand back  to aaron  thank you dr carling for that  informative presentation we will now  start the live q a portion of our  webinar and if you have any questions  you want to ask please do so now just  click on the ask a question box located  on the far left of your screen  so let's get started it looks like we  already have some great questions coming  in  our first question  what did you learn from the moonshot  project  um that's a good question i learned a  lot i think  i learned about peptide analysis which  um i found really useful and quite  excited about  and i believe that in the states you do  a lot more with peptides already in the  routine clinical laboratory than we do  so i think that that's an area that we  would like to really expand not  necessarily expand into but start  looking at in more detail and i'd  actually be keen to hear from anyone  who's got more experience than we do  with peptide analysis but i think also  learnt a lot about working as part of a  team because there was obviously quite a  lot of people involved in moonshot  everybody had um an individual role but  we also needed to work together and on  top of that we were kind of  geographically dispersed across  the whole of the uk so that was quite  challenging so yeah it was definitely a  steep learning curve but it was a very  enjoyable experience  thank you so much and dr crawling will  the method be able to detect the new  variants  um so that's a very good question so  obviously um it depends on what the new  variant is so until the delta variant  came along the method wasn't affected by  any of the mutations that we were aware  of um so for example the temperature the  kent mutate the kent variant and the  california the south african and  brazilian i think all of those mutations  were predominantly on the spike protein  so they didn't affect our encap peptides  so in the event that we get a variant  like the delta variant which does affect  the encap peptide the first question is  whether it affects one of our target  peptides  and then as with the delta variant um  it's about whereabouts from the target  peptide it is and how that affects the  binding to the ciscapper so we were  lucky really with delta in the sense  that although it did affect the ay ade  peptide um the ciscapper was owned the  affinity was only decreased by about 20  so we still captured the peptide  successfully and i guess that's the  beauty of mass spec because just by  updating our mrms to reflect the change  in the peptide we were able to still  detect the delta variant and because  obviously we have got three peptides in  the method um i guess it would be not  impossible but unfortunate if a mutation  came a variant came along which had  mutations which affected all three  peptides at the same time so i guess  worst case scenario you may lose one but  hopefully the presence of the other two  still allow you to detect the covid  thank you so much and i want to thank  our audience for these great questions  our next question why did you compare to  the off diagnostics method when  validating the kit and not the  attack  asset  yeah so um that was the way the project  works so in the first part of the  project as part of the moon shot and we  we'd already got an agreement to do the  um comparison with the crick laboratory  um in london and they were using the  tacpath assay so when we went on to  validate the waters kit  that was just done in my center which is  um symptoms of hospital in london so we  had to validate the pcr essay and we had  to validate against our own pcr essay  and that our biology department were  using our diagnostics um i was a bit  perturbed at first because  the old diagnostics assay measures  things in terms of takeoff value not ct  so that took a little bit of getting our  head around but i think in terms of the  practicalities um and the um the  validity of the validation um it doesn't  really make any difference because you  can't compare but my understanding is  anyway that you can't necessarily  compare the ct values from one pcr assay  to another anyway um so yeah it's just  the way that things worked out for us  thank you so much and we have one more  question coming in can you explain the  criteria used to classify results as  either positive or negative  yes so so this was um  this was probably one of the most  interesting aspects of the project  because one of our jobs as the p2  laboratory was to work out exactly how  to do that so we were measuring the  three target peptides and for each  peptide we had a quantifier iron and two  qualifier ions so we would look  individually just visually at each  peptide to check that the quantifier and  the qualifiers were there and if they  were we would class that as positive for  that individual peptide but then we  would look collectively at the three  peptides and we would want to have two  out of the three present before we would  um classify the sample as positive  and then we were lucky because um as i  mentioned earlier we were able to send  the data um on to  hans vistas at water who looked at it  retrospectively  and used much more uh sophisticated  techniques than me sat in front of the  mass spec looking at the screen and he  actually came back and validated what we  found um using sort of um more  sophisticated algorithms so yeah so that  was good and again for me that was one  of the learning points of the project  because certainly in the uk the way we  process our data post analytically is  often quite basic and i think that  there's um there's things that we can  take forward and learn here  and apply to some of our other assays my  um  day job is metabolic biochemistry so i'd  like to see how this could look with  atop antenes and amino acids things like  that as well  dr carlene this has been a pleasure  thank you for your presentation today do  you have any closing remarks you'd like  to provide for our audience before we go  um no just thank you very much for  inviting me to speak to you today i hope  you found the talk interesting and if  anybody would does have experience of  peptides analysis in the clinical  laboratory i'd really like to hear from  you and hear about how you've got on and  what analytes you've been measuring  that'd be really useful for me  well thank you again dr carling and  thank you for your time and for your  important research and before we go i  want to thank our audience for joining  us today and for their interesting  questions i'd also like to thank our  sponsor waters corporation for  sponsoring today's webinar questions we  did not have time for today and those  submitted during the on-demand period  will be addressed by our speaker via the  contact information you provided at the  time of registration and this webcast  can be viewed on demand for two years  until november 2023 laboratory alert via  email when it's available for replay we  encourage you to share that email with  your colleagues who may have missed  today's live event until next time take  care everyone stay healthy bye
 

